* ---- bench: offset_mc (klt sim port of sim/comparator-offset-transient-mc)
*
* Whole-latch input-referred offset, one Monte-Carlo draw per klt sim
* sample. Circuit is sim/comparator-offset-transient-mc/testbench/
* tb_offset_transient_mc.spice's (a single comparator_dut driven by a
* 33-level, -48 ... +48 mV, 3 mV/step, 30 ns/step differential staircase),
* carried over element for element, with one structural change forced by
* klt sim's supply axis: the clock is a unit pulse scaled by the live
* supply node (Bclk), because `alter vsup=<V>` does not reach the original
* `pulse(0 {vdd_val} ...)` amplitude.
*
* WHAT MOVES FROM THE DECK INTO klt sim. The original bench ran its 60
* draws inside one ngspice process (`setseed 20260916`, a `dowhile` loop of
* `reset` + `tran`) and reduced them in-deck. Here klt sim owns the draw
* loop: `monte_carlo.n` samples per corner, each a separate ngspice process
* whose `.options seed=` card klt derives from `monte_carlo.seed` (klt's
* SHA-256 seed contract), and the per-draw offset is one `.meas ... param=`
* in the request. The mismatch switch is still the corner section
* (`mos_<p>_mismatch` of cornerMOSlv.lib), exactly as before. The draws
* are therefore a different realization of the same statistical basis
* (whole latch, mismatch models, N per point), not a bit-identical replay.

.options reltol=1e-4 vntol=1e-9 abstol=1e-13

.param c_route=10f

vsup vdd 0 dc 1.2

vcm cm 0 dc {dut_vcm}
vd   vd 0 pwl(
+ 0.0000n -0.048 29.8000n -0.048 30.0000n -0.045 59.8000n -0.045
+ 60.0000n -0.042 89.8000n -0.042 90.0000n -0.039 119.8000n -0.039
+ 120.0000n -0.036 149.8000n -0.036 150.0000n -0.033 179.8000n -0.033
+ 180.0000n -0.03 209.8000n -0.03 210.0000n -0.027 239.8000n -0.027
+ 240.0000n -0.024 269.8000n -0.024 270.0000n -0.021 299.8000n -0.021
+ 300.0000n -0.018 329.8000n -0.018 330.0000n -0.015 359.8000n -0.015
+ 360.0000n -0.012 389.8000n -0.012 390.0000n -0.009 419.8000n -0.009
+ 420.0000n -0.006 449.8000n -0.006 450.0000n -0.003 479.8000n -0.003
+ 480.0000n 0 509.8000n 0 510.0000n 0.003 539.8000n 0.003
+ 540.0000n 0.006 569.8000n 0.006 570.0000n 0.009 599.8000n 0.009
+ 600.0000n 0.012 629.8000n 0.012 630.0000n 0.015 659.8000n 0.015
+ 660.0000n 0.018 689.8000n 0.018 690.0000n 0.021 719.8000n 0.021
+ 720.0000n 0.024 749.8000n 0.024 750.0000n 0.027 779.8000n 0.027
+ 780.0000n 0.03 809.8000n 0.03 810.0000n 0.033 839.8000n 0.033
+ 840.0000n 0.036 869.8000n 0.036 870.0000n 0.039 899.8000n 0.039
+ 900.0000n 0.042 929.8000n 0.042 930.0000n 0.045 959.8000n 0.045
+ 960.0000n 0.048 989.8000n 0.048 990.0000n 0.048
+ )
Eap ap cm vd 0 0.5
Ean an cm vd 0 -0.5

* one strobe per 30 ns step (original: pulse(0 {vdd_val} 10n 100p 100p 10n
* 30n)), scaled by the altered supply
vclku clku 0 pulse(0 1 10n 100p 100p 10n 30n)
Bclk  clk  0 v = 'v(clku)*v(vdd)'

ib   vdd ibn dc {dut_ib}
Xa   ap an clk ibn dout doutb vdd 0 comparator_dut
Cwd  dout 0 {c_route}

Bdoutn doutn 0 v = 'v(dout)/v(vdd)'

* ---- corner-application probe (see regeneration bench) ------------------
Btchk tcheck 0 v = 'temper'

* ---- per-level decision reads (as in the original tb.json analyses) ------
* One `find` per staircase level, 3 ns before that level's step ends (the
* original deck's `meas tran dlevK find v(doutn) at=...` lines, verbatim
* times). They live in the BODY, not the request, only to keep the
* envelope small (2700 samples x 33 reads); every value still prints to the
* per-sample ngspice log, and the request's `lowcount`/`vos_mv` measurements
* are computed from them by ngspice itself (`.meas ... param=`).
.meas tran dlev0 find v(doutn) at=27.0000n
.meas tran dlev1 find v(doutn) at=57.0000n
